#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172885} :activity/id #uuid "81dd1d17-811a-52af-9ad3-b3247253617b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050760 :topic/id #uuid "64670a5e-dd35-51fc-bd24-210590f74fcd" :topic/math-academy-id 3336} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173152 :tutorial/content "The general equation of a second order (quadratic) curve is an expression of the form\n\n$$\\alpha_{11}x_1^2+2\\alpha_{12}x_1x_2 + \\alpha_{22}x_2^2 + 2\\alpha_{13}x_1+ 2\\alpha_{23}x_2 +\\alpha_{33}=0,$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are variables, and $\\alpha_{ij} \\in \\Bbb R$ for all $1\\leq i,j \\leq 3.$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$3x_1^2-x_1x_2-5x_2^2+5x_2 - 1 = 0$$\n\nis a general quadratic curve." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d9b3527-1999-5169-affa-96a37f99fb01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8cf7cde9-4168-5bb5-a77b-f1c02aa56436" :step/math-academy-id 31225 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171834 :question/id #uuid "1ecd84f8-5157-595a-93b6-a865acaa53d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8378"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550002}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"} :step/id #uuid "b2f2a127-b40d-5b8f-ba64-4a1cad0e08c9" :step/math-academy-id 31226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173153 :tutorial/content "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind $$\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,$$ where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as\n\n$$\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables $$\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}$$ and, by completing the square for the variable $y_2$, we get the equation\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as $$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix},$$ which is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which both semiaxes are equal to $2.$\n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system." :tutorial/id #uuid "cd5dde1f-7d5c-58d9-8674-6bf984a51d0d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5005 :tutorial/title "Classifying a General Quadratic Curve"} :step/id #uuid "e347e125-d8fa-58ef-8dc4-81fc67d191f3" :step/math-academy-id 31227 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171835 :question/id #uuid "c03c6fbb-176a-5f30-b928-73908cb93277" :question/math-academy-id "e-8380"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366718}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"} :step/id #uuid "3eb3af03-a01d-5d40-94ed-828350b2cacf" :step/math-academy-id 31229 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171836 :question/id #uuid "58f6a3e7-e867-576a-ad84-0debbbc38d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-8381"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404871}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"} :step/id #uuid "8393be76-0567-5bf7-bd97-bfad1ef56506" :step/math-academy-id 31230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173155 :tutorial/content "A quadratic equation with no cross-terms can be rewritten in standard form by completing the square. Let's see how this is done in the following example.\n\nSuppose we are given the equation\n\n$$2x_1^2+x_2^2 - 4x_1 + 4x_2 = 2,$$\n\nwhat type of curve does it represent?\n\nFirst, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nwe get the following equation in standard form:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bd1f3565-37df-5db7-aa06-a573b6b58f8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5015 :tutorial/title "Classifying a Quadratic Curve That Does Not Contain a Cross-Product Term"} :step/id #uuid "ea16c9e4-6358-52d5-9437-e5c11b9cdeec" :step/math-academy-id 31358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173165 :tutorial/content "There are three basic so-called *non-degenerate quadratic curves*:\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Ellipse |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Hyperbola |\n| $x_2^2= 2px_1$ | Parabola |" :tutorial/id #uuid "31519439-ac97-5940-b01b-de36561f9618" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5129 :tutorial/title "Non-Degenerate Quadratic Curves"} :step/id #uuid "1e64c954-a22d-5a07-9a29-b00dae41d7a4" :step/math-academy-id 32209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173166 :tutorial/content "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Pair of<br> intersecting lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of <br> parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of <br> coincident lines |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7fa5c07c-714f-555e-9253-e8c6b56484e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5130 :tutorial/title "Degenerate (Real) Quadratic Curves"} :step/id #uuid "e4b30b5f-3e46-516c-bdb2-28d2099fba5a" :step/math-academy-id 32210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173167 :tutorial/content "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary<br> parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,$ which is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81eb37e3-202d-5934-93ce-8534c3ffc1a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5131 :tutorial/title "Empty Sets"} :step/id #uuid "980cb0f2-0635-5e7b-926e-f8cbd302bf7b" :step/math-academy-id 32211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172888}}] :activity/title "Classifying Quadratic Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172167}]}
